m, bare aluminum and diamond plate can all appear on one body.</li>\n<li><strong>Gear on top.</strong> Ladder racks, pipe carriers, cranes and compressors sit above the body and are bolted through it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<figure><img src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/Apex-Property-Managment-Ford-F150-White-Decal_DSC09271-1024x538.webp\" alt=\"White Ford F-150 work truck with cut vinyl decals, the lettering approach that suits service body truck wraps\" width=\"1024\" height=\"538\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>On many work trucks, clean cut lettering on the cab and body doors does more than a full wrap would.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Which Surfaces Take a Service Body Truck Wrap</h2>\n<p>The manufacturers'' substrate bulletins sort service body materials cleanly.</p>\n<h3>Painted steel and painted aluminum</h3>\n<p>Factory-painted body panels in good condition are standard wrap surfaces, prepared like any painted panel. The caution is rust: steel bodies rust first at hinges, door bottoms and where tools have chipped the paint. 3M is explicit that film must never go directly on carbon steel; corrosion has to be removed and the panel primed or painted first. Our <a href=\"/rust-repair-before-vehicle-wrap-toronto/\">rust repair guide</a> covers the sequence.</p>\n<h3>Bare aluminum</h3>\n<p>Many utility bodies are unpainted aluminum. Both 3M and Avery Dennison prefer etched or anodised aluminum, and both say bare aluminum that has oxidised must have the oxidation removed and be degreased before film is applied. Smooth bare aluminum can be wrapped once prepared; weathered, chalky aluminum needs work first, and a shop should test adhesion before committing to a whole body.</p>\n<h3>Diamond plate and textured surfaces</h3>\n<p>Diamond plate (tread plate) cannot be wrapped. Its raised pattern is far rougher than the 150-grit smoothness 3M sets as the practical limit for adhesive contact, and film laid over it will bridge the pattern and lift. The same applies to spray-on bed liner on body tops and to textured plastic fender flares. Design around these areas or use lettering on the flat panels only.</p>\n<h3>Rubber seals, gaskets and sealant</h3>\n<p>Every compartment door has a rubber seal and most body joints carry sealant. 3M lists rubber and caulking materials as surfaces film does not reliably adhere to, so film stops at the painted edge of each door.</p>\n\n<h2>Designing Around the Doors</h2>\n<p>The compartment doors decide the layout. Three principles make a service body truck wrap read well:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Treat each door as a tile.</strong> A graphic that flows across several doors is cut by every gap, latch and hinge line. Backgrounds and colour fields can flow; text and logos should sit whole on a single door or on the cab.</li>\n<li><strong>Put the words where there is no hardware.</strong> The cab doors are the largest uninterrupted panels on the truck and the natural home for the company name and phone number. Large upper compartment doors come next. Keep lettering off latches, lock cylinders and reflectors.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the rear.</strong> The rear compartment doors and the tailgate or bumper area are what drivers behind the truck see. A phone number and web address belong there, sized to be read from a car length back.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For the trades that run these trucks, the message hierarchy is the same one that works on vans: trade first, name second, contact third. Our articles on <a href=\"/plumbing-and-hvac-van-wraps-toronto/\">plumbing and HVAC van wraps</a> and <a href=\"/fleet-decals-for-contractors/\">fleet decals for contractors</a> go into what converts.</p>\n\n<h2>Wrap or Lettering?</h2>\n<p>On a service body truck wrap project, cut vinyl lettering and decals are often the better answer, not the budget compromise. Lettering has no large sheets to lift at door edges, each piece sits on one panel, a damaged letter is replaced on its own, and the many colours of a utility body (white cab, grey compartments, aluminum trim) are left to do their job. A printed wrap earns its place on the cab, where there are large smooth panels, and on a painted body with a few big doors. A common and durable layout is a <a href=\"/partial-truck-wrap-toronto-2/\">partial truck wrap</a> on the cab with <a href=\"/truck-lettering-and-decals-toronto/\">truck lettering and decals</a> on the body.</p>\n<p>If the body is bare aluminum, the decision is simpler still: lettering and logo decals on the prepared aluminum, with the cab wrapped or lettered to match.</p>\n\n<figure><img src=\"/wp-content/uploads/2026/05/Aimed-Group_Ford_F250_Partial-Wrap_Commercial_Vinyl-Wrap-Toronto_Before-Image-1024x538.webp\" alt=\"White Ford F-250 work truck before graphics, the typical starting point for a service body truck wrap\" width=\"1024\" height=\"538\" loading=\"lazy\" /><figcaption>Most service trucks arrive white. The question is which panels earn film and which earn lettering.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h2>Racks, Cranes and the Gear on Top</h2>\n<p>A service body truck wrap also has to account for what sits on top. Ladder racks and pipe carriers bolt through the body top, and their feet sit on the surface the wrap would cover. Where the design runs across the top rails, the rack comes off before wrapping and goes back on afterwards; where it does not, the film ends at the rack feet. Cranes, compressors and inverters bring heat, hydraulic fluid and fuel into the picture, and film near them should be kept clear of exhaust outlets and fluid drips. Decide all of this before the truck arrives; our guide to <a href=\"/prepare-your-truck-for-a-wrap/\">preparing a truck for a wrap</a> covers the accessories conversation.</p>\n\n<h2>Regulatory Lettering</h2>\n<p>Service trucks often operate under commercial rules, and Ontario''s Highway Traffic Act requires commercial motor vehicles above certain weights to show the owner''s name on both sides. Unit numbers, CVOR details and safety markings may also apply depending on the fleet. Design them in at the start, on a flat door, in a durable cut vinyl, rather than adding stickers afterwards. Our <a href=\"/fleet-decals-ontario-compliance-guide/\">fleet decal compliance guide</a> covers the practical side; confirm your own obligations with the Ministry of Transportation.</p>\n\n<h2>Durability Expectations on a Working Body</h2>\n<p>Be realistic about how a service body truck wrap wears. Film on compartment doors is handled, scraped by tools and pressure-washed at the edges in a way that film on a car door never is. Edges near latches and door bottoms will show wear first, and the lower band collects salt and gravel. That is an argument for designing so the high-wear pieces are small and replaceable: a lettering set or a single door panel can be redone in an hour, where a full side wrap cannot. Schedule a walk-around every season, and read our <a href=\"/fleet-wrap-replacement-schedule-guide/\">fleet wrap replacement schedule</a> for how often working fleets refresh their graphics.</p>\n\n<h2>Brand Your Service Trucks</h2>\n<p>Vinyl Wrap Toronto wraps and letters service bodies, pickups and vans for trades and fleets across the GTA in 3M and Avery Dennison films, with each body inspected and each door planned before any film is cut. See our <a href=\"/commercial-vehicle-wraps/\">commercial vehicle wraps</a> and <a href=\"/truck-wraps/\">truck wraps</a> pages, then <a href=\"/contact/\">request a quote</a> or call <a href=\"tel:416-746-1381\">416-746-1381</a>.</p>\n\n<p><em>Sources: 3M Instruction Bulletin, <a href=\"https://multimedia.3m.com/mws/media/2419614O/3m-instruction-bulletin-application-substrate-selection-and-preparation-ra-pdf.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Application: Substrate Selection and Preparation</a> (aluminum, carbon steel, rubber and caulking, surface smoothness); Avery Dennison <a href=\"https://graphics.averydennison.com/content/dam/averydennison/graphics/ap/en/Instructional-Bulletins/ib-1.01-substrate-cleaning-and-preparation-ap-en.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noreferrer noopener\">Instructional Bulletin 1.01</a> (aluminum preparation).</em></p>\n","style":"text-align:start;font-family:\"Poppins\", Sans-serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.5em;font-weight:300"}]},{"width":25,"background":"#F8F8F8","padding":"10px 10px 10px 10px","blocks":[{"type":"toc","title":"Table of Contents","box":"background-color:#F8F8F8"},{"type":"form"}]}]}]}]', datetime('now')
